## Boundaries

- Keep external boundary parsing close to HTTP handlers, CLI args, env readers, file readers, queue consumers, and provider SDK wrappers.
- Keep business logic out of transport glue when practical.
- Keep generated API/types/codegen artifacts out of manual edits unless repo docs say otherwise.
- For monorepos, treat shared schema, public API, worker, runtime, and client changes as contract work. Update all consumers together.

## Search Scope

When grepping, finding, or reading within the repo, exclude dependency, cache, and build output. They pollute results, slow `find`, and hold no source-of-truth content.

- `node_modules/`
- `dist/`
- `build/`
- `coverage/`
- `.next/`, `.vite/`, `.turbo/`, `.cache/`
- `.tsbuildinfo`

Examples:

```bash
rg --hidden -g '!{node_modules,dist,build,coverage,.next,.vite,.turbo,.cache}/**' '<pattern>'
find . -type d \( -name node_modules -o -name dist -o -name build -o -name coverage \) -prune -o -print
```

Metadata reads inside excluded dirs are fine when the file itself is the source of truth (lockfiles, vendored manifests).

## Settings

All environment variables and CLI args enter the app through a single typed config object. Domain code never reads `process.env` (or `import.meta.env` in browser code) directly. Construct once at startup, pass explicitly downstream.

- One config module owns parsing and validation (Zod or equivalent). Treat unknown env as untrusted input.
- Never branch on raw `process.env.X` in route handlers, services, or components. Read from the typed config.
- Tests build the config from overrides rather than mutating `process.env` globally.

